Sofía found the spec Thursday night at 11:40 and sent it with no mes‐
sage at all, which from Sofía is a siren.
   It was a scan, crooked, with the staple holes still in it. EARLY CALM
PILOT — EQUIPMENT SPECIFICATION. Two items were circled in
red pen.

   Item 4. Ambient Calming Tone (“Tone A”). Carrier: 110 Hz. Pulse rate: 6.5 Hz.
   Cycle: 8 seconds, repeating. Level: below conversation. Placement: above
   storage, out of student sightline.
   Item 5. Acoustic treatment. Ceiling baffles, door seals, window film. Target noise
   floor: 30 dB or lower.

In the margin, in blue ballpoint, somebody who had actually been in
those classrooms had written who approved this?? Kids need to MOVE.
Nobody had answered.
    I read Item 5 four times before I understood why it made me sick.
    They hadn’t only put a tone in the room. They’d taken the room out
first. Every classroom in the pilot had been sanded down to a whisper,
quiet enough to hear a pin land, and then they’d put one sound in the
hole. A kid who runs on noise, who needs the world loud to hear him‐
self think, sits down in that room and everything goes flat, and the only
thing he has left to push back with is his own hand on a desk leg.




